Biomass estimation: Train a regression model (Random Forest or a CNN like U-Net) on NDVI/canopy-height time series to estimate above-ground biomass change — this is the standard remote-sensing carbon MRV (Measurement, Reporting, Verification) approach.


Fraud/anomaly detection: Compare claimed project boundaries against actual imagery-detected vegetation change; flag mismatches, "double-counting" (same land registered twice), or edited imagery using an isolation forest or autoencoder trained on legitimate project patterns.


Stack: Python, TensorFlow/PyTorch, rasterio/geopandas for geospatial preprocessing. Serve inference via a FastAPI microservice.


Output: a verified "tonnes CO2 sequestered" number with a confidence score, which becomes the input to minting.